2026-09-23T22:14:52Z starting M1 (issues #1-#4: version model, release discovery, download+SHA-256, GUI/state machine) because milestone M1 is the only deliverable milestone in the #1->#7 chain

2026-09-24 M1 delivered: pe-bear/updater/ implements update discovery, compatible-asset
selection, managed-installation detection, opt-in download, streaming SHA-256 verification,
settings, the update dialog and the state machine up to ReadyToInstall. Installation itself
is not attempted: requestInstall() records consent and reports ErrorInstallerUnavailable,
which is M2/M3 work.

Deviations from the plan, all deliberate:
- PR #1 planned a separate "test(updater)" commit. Tests ship in the commit with the code
  they cover instead, so that every commit builds and passes on its own.
- The build-time package descriptors (PEBEAR_PACKAGE_TYPE, PEBEAR_BUILD_RUNTIME,
  PEBEAR_MIN_OS_VERSION) were pulled forward from PR #7 into M1: asset selection cannot
  work without the build declaring what it is. The pe-bear-build.json manifest and the CI
  that would set these for published packages remain M4.
- Minimal CMake wiring was necessary to build M1 at all; full packaging integration is M4.
- A build that does not declare PEBEAR_PACKAGE_TYPE degrades to notify-only, by design.

2026-10-04 addendum, written while landing M1 step 4 of 4:
- Build/test CI now exists (Linux, macOS, Windows) and runs the suites on every push and
  pull request. It still does not set PEBEAR_PACKAGE_TYPE / PEBEAR_BUILD_RUNTIME /
  PEBEAR_MIN_OS_VERSION for published artifacts, so asset selection on a released build
  still degrades to notify-only. That wiring, and pe-bear-build.json, remain M4.
- The PE-bear test suite landed before this stack. Because base/MainSettings.cpp carries the
  updater's settings once the updater is built, every test target that compiles it has to
  link pebear_update_core. Neither change triggers this on its own; only the two together.

2026-10-04T21:29:19Z starting #18 (M2 part 1/3): transaction model and journal, with platform operations behind an interface so the state machine and rollback ordering are testable without a Windows VM

2026-10-04 #18 delivered: model transakcji i journal. TransactionState od Prepared przez
BackedUp, Activated, Validated do Committed/RolledBack/Failed; journal jeden plik na próbę,
zapisywany całościowo przez rename, więc po przerwaniu czytelny jest albo stary rekord, albo
nowy, nigdy mieszanka. Rollback odtwarza kroki w odwrotnej kolejności i kontynuuje po
nieudanym kroku, kończąc jako RolledBack tylko gdy wszystkie się udały. planRecovery jest
czystą funkcją rekordu.

Decyzja projektowa z #5 zrealizowana: wszystkie operacje plikowe idą przez IFileSystem.
FakeFileSystem w testach pozwala wstrzyknąć awarię w dowolnym kroku i w dowolnym wywołaniu,
czego na prawdziwym systemie plików nie da się zrobić powtarzalnie. Dzięki temu całe #18 jest
pokryte natywnie, bez Windows.
